  "configuration_id": "config_008",
  "reason": "Evidence: The best-performing configuration so far is config_005 (rna_only, multiprofile, scvi_n200, avgpool) at 0.650 acc / 0.628 F1, with config_006 (transformer aggregation) essentially matching it at 0.653. Image-only models are very weak (0.208–0.227), and the scvi_n200 encoder clearly outperforms pca_n2000 in both single- and multi-profile settings. However, the scgpt encoder has not been tested at all, leaving a significant gap in our understanding of which RNA representation is best. Question: config_008 (rna_only, multiprofile, scgpt, avgpool) is a clean single-variable comparison against config_005—the only difference is the RNA encoder (scgpt vs. scvi_n200), while profile setting and aggregation are held at their best-known values. This directly tests whether scgpt is competitive with or superior to scvi_n200 in the multiprofile regime where gains are largest. Why this is the best next experiment: (1) It resolves a major uncertainty about encoder choice before committing budget to multimodal or further aggregation experiments, since the encoder decision will affect all downstream multimodal configurations. (2) It follows the principle of isolating one variable: only the encoder changes relative to the current best. (3) If scgpt matches or exceeds scvi_n200, it opens a new promising branch; if it falls well short, we can confidently rule it out and focus remaining budget on multimodal fusion with scvi_n200. (4) Given that avgpool and transformer aggregation are nearly equivalent for scvi_n200, using avgpool here avoids confounding the encoder question with the aggregation choice."
}

